# Vulkan, both memory arrangements, 300 frames at 1920x1080, 2026-11-27
#
# Why this exists
#   The template has two ways to get a frame to the shader - one host-visible
#   buffer, or a device-local buffer with a host-visible staging copy - and
#   vulkan-compute.md quoted a 20-frame run for them. Twenty frames is enough to
#   see the shape and not enough to quote: the first runs of a day measure cold
#   pages, and a 300-frame average is the number a reader can reproduce.
#
# Machine: Windows 11, NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060, Vulkan 1.4.357 from the driver,
# MSVC 14.50, Vulkan-Headers + glslang 16.6.0 in a VULKAN_SDK-shaped directory
# (no LunarG SDK installed).
#
# Command (the suite grew --frames for exactly this; the default stays 20 so the
# test step stays quick):
#
#   VULKAN_SDK=... templates\minimal-vulkan-ofx\build.bat Release
#   build\self-test\vulkan_suite.exe --frames 300
#
# Frame: 1920x1080 RGBA32F, i.e. 2,073,600 pixels x 16 bytes = 33.18 MB one way,
# 66.4 MB per frame both ways. Warm-up frame before each measurement; the run
# reuses one buffer per size and per arrangement, so the per-frame path is a
# memcpy, one submit and one fence wait.
#
# Run A:
#   device: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 (Vulkan 1.4.357, queue family 0, 4294967040 px per dispatch)
#     host-visible   102.49 ms/frame total, dispatch 13.40, copies 89.05 (1 GB/s over both directions)
#     device-local   117.32 ms/frame total, dispatch 27.88, copies 89.39 (1 GB/s over both directions)
#     CPU              3.02 ms/frame (the loop render() falls back to)
#   vulkan_suite: 26 checks, 0 failed
#
# Run B (same binary, immediately after):
#     host-visible   102.58 ms/frame total, dispatch 13.39, copies 89.15 (1 GB/s over both directions)
#     device-local   117.17 ms/frame total, dispatch 28.11, copies 89.00 (1 GB/s over both directions)
#     CPU              3.00 ms/frame (the loop render() falls back to)
#   vulkan_suite: 26 checks, 0 failed
#
# What the before/after says, and it is not the answer the arrangement was added
# for:
#   - device-local + staging is 117.2-117.3 ms/frame against host-visible's
#     102.5-102.6 ms/frame, i.e. ~14% SLOWER at this size on this machine;
#   - the reason is visible in the columns: the CPU-side copies are ~89 ms in
#     both (66.4 MB per frame through mapped memory, ~0.75 GB/s counting both
#     directions), and device-local adds two vkCmdCopyBuffer trips over PCIe on
#     top, which is why its dispatch column reads 27.9-28.1 ms against 13.4 ms;
#   - so for ONE pass over a frame that must arrive and leave as CPU pixels,
#     staging makes the frame cross PCIe twice instead of once. Device-local is
#     the right arrangement when the frame stays resident and the per-pixel work
#     is large - the case this template does not model;
#   - both are 34x slower than the CPU loop (3.0 ms/frame), which is the number
#     that decides whether a Vulkan gain belongs in a plug-in at all.
#
# What this does not say
#   One GPU, one driver, one frame size, one frame per submit. No measurement of
#   a sequence that keeps a frame resident across passes, of a larger frame where
#   the dispatch would matter more, or of a second machine. The 20-frame run from
#   earlier the same day measured host-visible at 96.6-98.5 and device-local at
#   108.3-111.0 ms; the ordering is the same, the absolute numbers are ~5% lower,
#   which is why the documents quote this one and keep the other in
#   vulkan-suite.log.
